WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.502 --> 00:00:07.710
BRAD MEADER: My name is Brad.

00:00:07.710 --> 00:00:10.470
I'm the product lead for
Google endpoint management.

00:00:10.470 --> 00:00:13.650
I'm part of the Cloud Identity
product, so part of the Cloud

00:00:13.650 --> 00:00:14.452
Identity team.

00:00:14.452 --> 00:00:16.910
I'm going to try to keep it
high energy and keep you awake,

00:00:16.910 --> 00:00:18.307
here, for a little bit.

00:00:18.307 --> 00:00:19.890
I'm going to try not
to talk too much.

00:00:19.890 --> 00:00:21.390
We have some demos that
we're going to show you.

00:00:21.390 --> 00:00:23.223
So I going to bring up
some of my colleagues

00:00:23.223 --> 00:00:25.030
that are listed here
in a little bit.

00:00:25.030 --> 00:00:27.720
But first I want to
set a little context

00:00:27.720 --> 00:00:31.590
before we get into those demos.

00:00:31.590 --> 00:00:34.270
So I'll talk a little bit about
Cloud Identity in general.

00:00:34.270 --> 00:00:36.960
So, the idea here is that--

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:39.510
with the proliferation
of mobile devices,

00:00:39.510 --> 00:00:42.390
and the movement of enterprise
access to the cloud--

00:00:42.390 --> 00:00:46.110
it's kind of changed the
way that employees work.

00:00:46.110 --> 00:00:50.280
All of us expect the same
kind of consumer-grade access

00:00:50.280 --> 00:00:52.680
from our enterprise
applications.

00:00:52.680 --> 00:00:55.710
And it's actually
putting a burden on IT

00:00:55.710 --> 00:00:56.790
to support this, right?

00:00:56.790 --> 00:00:58.770
So the workplace is
undergoing a transformation

00:00:58.770 --> 00:01:01.230
to support this expectation.

00:01:01.230 --> 00:01:03.690
IT decision makers
are at the forefront

00:01:03.690 --> 00:01:06.240
of creating a secure,
connected, and collaborative

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:10.080
workplace where identity,
access, and device management

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:11.760
is front and center
of all of that.

00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:15.690
So, network security
was different

00:01:15.690 --> 00:01:18.492
when I started my career,
and so was collaboration.

00:01:18.492 --> 00:01:20.700
And I think those two are
kind of tied, hand-in-hand,

00:01:20.700 --> 00:01:21.870
together.

00:01:21.870 --> 00:01:24.990
As long as we stayed on the
network, we stayed secure.

00:01:24.990 --> 00:01:27.180
This tied us to our desks
and it hindered the way

00:01:27.180 --> 00:01:29.650
we could work with others.

00:01:29.650 --> 00:01:34.350
And so, in a way, that the IT
security model kind of dictated

00:01:34.350 --> 00:01:36.600
the way that we could
work with our partners,

00:01:36.600 --> 00:01:38.350
and our vendors,
and our customers.

00:01:38.350 --> 00:01:40.650
And so now that that
perimeter is gone,

00:01:40.650 --> 00:01:42.900
and we've got this dynamic
security posture tied

00:01:42.900 --> 00:01:45.090
to users and devices
and applications,

00:01:45.090 --> 00:01:46.950
the result is a
better way to work

00:01:46.950 --> 00:01:48.873
that allows us to easily
collaborate across

00:01:48.873 --> 00:01:50.790
different applications,
from different places,

00:01:50.790 --> 00:01:53.880
on different devices.

00:01:53.880 --> 00:01:55.460
Google pioneered this concept--

00:01:55.460 --> 00:01:57.080
we call it BeyondCorp--

00:01:57.080 --> 00:01:58.250
in 2011.

00:01:58.250 --> 00:02:02.300
We've been working on
this for eight years now.

00:02:02.300 --> 00:02:04.700
The intent has been to
enable every employee

00:02:04.700 --> 00:02:06.890
to work from untrusted
networks without the use

00:02:06.890 --> 00:02:08.720
of a traditional VPN.

00:02:08.720 --> 00:02:11.000
We've been perfecting
this internally

00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:12.590
for these eight years--

00:02:12.590 --> 00:02:14.090
shifting all of
our access controls

00:02:14.090 --> 00:02:16.820
from the network to
individual users and devices--

00:02:16.820 --> 00:02:18.570
allowing them to
work from anywhere.

00:02:18.570 --> 00:02:21.060
And so, what we've done,
is we've taken that,

00:02:21.060 --> 00:02:24.260
and we package it up, and we've
started selling that as part

00:02:24.260 --> 00:02:25.900
of our Cloud Identity product.

00:02:25.900 --> 00:02:28.340
Now, the Cloud Identity
product was officially

00:02:28.340 --> 00:02:31.550
launched in March of last
year, so it's about a year old.

00:02:31.550 --> 00:02:33.050
But it's not actually
a new product.

00:02:33.050 --> 00:02:35.330
It's something that we
had baked in as part

00:02:35.330 --> 00:02:36.943
of G Suite for years.

00:02:36.943 --> 00:02:39.110
But we finally thought, oh,
this is something that--

00:02:39.110 --> 00:02:41.060
even if you're not making
the move to the productivity

00:02:41.060 --> 00:02:41.810
suite--

00:02:41.810 --> 00:02:44.280
we think our customers
could benefit from.

00:02:44.280 --> 00:02:46.860
So, that's kind of the
overview of Cloud Identity.

00:02:46.860 --> 00:02:49.220
So what does device management
have to do with that?

00:02:49.220 --> 00:02:51.270
That's what I'm here to
talk about in more depth.

00:02:51.270 --> 00:02:52.850
So, first, let me
talk a little bit

00:02:52.850 --> 00:02:56.660
about what I think device
management is, or endpoint

00:02:56.660 --> 00:02:57.300
management.

00:02:57.300 --> 00:02:59.480
So, a primer, right?

00:02:59.480 --> 00:03:01.280
So I think of it
in three pillars.

00:03:01.280 --> 00:03:03.440
So I think of device
management itself,

00:03:03.440 --> 00:03:05.150
where you're locking
down a device.

00:03:05.150 --> 00:03:08.150
So you get an inventory
of every device

00:03:08.150 --> 00:03:10.070
that access is your data.

00:03:10.070 --> 00:03:11.690
You enforce policies
on that device

00:03:11.690 --> 00:03:14.510
before they are allowed access,
so you might enforce a passcode

00:03:14.510 --> 00:03:15.920
on a device.

00:03:15.920 --> 00:03:18.500
You want to have the ability
to take action on that device,

00:03:18.500 --> 00:03:21.320
if something happens, if the
data becomes the device becomes

00:03:21.320 --> 00:03:22.422
lost or stolen.

00:03:22.422 --> 00:03:24.380
So, basically, locking
down a device before you

00:03:24.380 --> 00:03:27.240
allow access to
sensitive information.

00:03:27.240 --> 00:03:30.080
The second pillar is
application management.

00:03:30.080 --> 00:03:32.570
So, once you've got a device
and it's locked down in a way

00:03:32.570 --> 00:03:34.185
that you feel like
it's secure, you

00:03:34.185 --> 00:03:36.560
want to make sure that your
users are productive on that.

00:03:36.560 --> 00:03:38.643
And so you want to make
sure that they have access

00:03:38.643 --> 00:03:40.520
to the right
applications and they're

00:03:40.520 --> 00:03:42.150
configured in the right way.

00:03:42.150 --> 00:03:45.440
And so things like
application whitelisting,

00:03:45.440 --> 00:03:48.290
applications that separate
out work and personal data,

00:03:48.290 --> 00:03:50.450
distributing applications
and configuring them--

00:03:50.450 --> 00:03:52.790
that sort of thing-- that's
the second pillar of what

00:03:52.790 --> 00:03:55.790
I consider enterprise
endpoint management.

00:03:55.790 --> 00:03:57.890
And then the third piece
is general administration

00:03:57.890 --> 00:03:59.060
of your fleet of devices.

00:03:59.060 --> 00:04:01.520
So you've got these
devices out there.

00:04:01.520 --> 00:04:03.690
You want to audit
and report on them.

00:04:03.690 --> 00:04:08.150
You want to be able to maintain
threat mitigation, protocol

00:04:08.150 --> 00:04:09.005
on that.

00:04:09.005 --> 00:04:10.970
And just general
enterprise management

00:04:10.970 --> 00:04:14.170
of your device fleet.

00:04:14.170 --> 00:04:16.540
So why is all of this important?

00:04:16.540 --> 00:04:20.350
So more than one quarter
of smartphone owners

00:04:20.350 --> 00:04:22.720
do not use a screen lock.

00:04:22.720 --> 00:04:26.932
So this is a couple
of years old here.

00:04:26.932 --> 00:04:28.390
When you dig into
that data, that's

00:04:28.390 --> 00:04:30.820
not limited to consumers.

00:04:30.820 --> 00:04:34.390
So that means that a quarter
of all folks carrying

00:04:34.390 --> 00:04:36.430
a modern smartphone
have figured out

00:04:36.430 --> 00:04:39.350
how to turn off screen
lock on their device.

00:04:39.350 --> 00:04:42.100
I don't know if you've set
up a smartphone recently,

00:04:42.100 --> 00:04:43.240
it's hard to do.

00:04:43.240 --> 00:04:48.080
So you've made the
decision that, I

00:04:48.080 --> 00:04:51.067
don't want to be bothered with
a device lock on my device.

00:04:51.067 --> 00:04:53.650
And I would just want it to be
open for the world to get into.

00:04:56.350 --> 00:05:01.390
And then depending on the
data that you look at,

00:05:01.390 --> 00:05:03.970
only about one in
10, or maybe 15%,

00:05:03.970 --> 00:05:09.100
of devices used for business
use are under device management.

00:05:09.100 --> 00:05:10.750
If they're not under
device management,

00:05:10.750 --> 00:05:13.030
then you don't know if
you've enforced a screen

00:05:13.030 --> 00:05:14.993
lock on that device or not.

00:05:14.993 --> 00:05:16.660
So, this is what I'm
here to say, right?

00:05:16.660 --> 00:05:19.300
If you're an
administrator and you're

00:05:19.300 --> 00:05:22.938
allowing enterprise access to
your data on a mobile device,

00:05:22.938 --> 00:05:24.730
and you don't have
device management turned

00:05:24.730 --> 00:05:27.670
on that device,
then your employees

00:05:27.670 --> 00:05:30.490
can leave their
device at a Starbucks.

00:05:30.490 --> 00:05:33.340
And it could potentially
be completely open.

00:05:33.340 --> 00:05:34.720
Somebody can pick
up that device,

00:05:34.720 --> 00:05:37.210
and they can get complete access
to whatever sensitive data

00:05:37.210 --> 00:05:38.530
resides in the device.

00:05:38.530 --> 00:05:42.190
Not only is that bad for the
potential loss of your data,

00:05:42.190 --> 00:05:45.620
you could run afoul of
security regulations as well.

00:05:45.620 --> 00:05:49.450
So I think that is bad,
and so I'm on a mission

00:05:49.450 --> 00:05:50.680
to try to fix that.

00:05:50.680 --> 00:05:54.380
So, why do I think that is?

00:05:54.380 --> 00:06:00.650
So, the reality is, it's been
difficult to setup device

00:06:00.650 --> 00:06:02.330
management.

00:06:02.330 --> 00:06:05.510
You send people
through a lot of hoops.

00:06:05.510 --> 00:06:09.300
There are a lot of privacy
concerns to set it up.

00:06:09.300 --> 00:06:11.240
And so, in general,
people don't want

00:06:11.240 --> 00:06:14.570
to deal with the headaches of
implementing device management.

00:06:14.570 --> 00:06:17.570
And so, at Google,
where we're on a mission

00:06:17.570 --> 00:06:18.435
to try to fix that.

00:06:18.435 --> 00:06:20.060
We're on a mission
to try to figure out

00:06:20.060 --> 00:06:22.790
how to make device management
as easy as possible,

00:06:22.790 --> 00:06:27.090
as seamless as possible, so
that as an administrator,

00:06:27.090 --> 00:06:28.940
you don't have a
problem turning it on.

00:06:28.940 --> 00:06:30.560
And as an end user,
you don't have

00:06:30.560 --> 00:06:34.250
a problem having device
management on your device.

00:06:34.250 --> 00:06:37.300
So our mission is to
maximize user productivity

00:06:37.300 --> 00:06:39.880
while keeping data secure.

00:06:39.880 --> 00:06:41.980
These two things
are diametrically

00:06:41.980 --> 00:06:43.810
opposed to each
other, all right?

00:06:43.810 --> 00:06:47.770
So it's really easy to
keep your data secure.

00:06:47.770 --> 00:06:52.240
You lock it down, you don't give
anybody access, done, right?

00:06:52.240 --> 00:06:54.520
It's really easy to
keep people productive.

00:06:54.520 --> 00:06:56.690
They can get access to your
data anytime, anywhere.

00:06:56.690 --> 00:07:00.760
You just open it wide
for access anywhere,

00:07:00.760 --> 00:07:02.480
but then it's not secure.

00:07:02.480 --> 00:07:04.000
So we're constantly
looking at what

00:07:04.000 --> 00:07:07.660
is the right balance
between those two things.

00:07:07.660 --> 00:07:13.250
I think we've achieved a pretty
good balance between those two.

00:07:13.250 --> 00:07:16.650
At Google-- our
endpoint management--

00:07:16.650 --> 00:07:22.170
we manage more than 55
million 30 day active devices.

00:07:22.170 --> 00:07:25.920
That includes Chrome
OS, Android, iOS,

00:07:25.920 --> 00:07:28.350
and some other devices.

00:07:28.350 --> 00:07:30.810
We've managed to
grow pretty rapidly

00:07:30.810 --> 00:07:32.470
over the last few years.

00:07:32.470 --> 00:07:36.460
And we've done that
by making simple,

00:07:36.460 --> 00:07:37.905
easy to use device management.

00:07:37.905 --> 00:07:40.530
And those are the demos that I'm
going to show you a little bit

00:07:40.530 --> 00:07:44.010
later, I want to show
you how we've done that.

00:07:44.010 --> 00:07:48.540
In many places, we've turned on
device management by default.

00:07:48.540 --> 00:07:51.327
So that we've got basically--
even for administrators

00:07:51.327 --> 00:07:53.160
who don't know they
need device management--

00:07:53.160 --> 00:07:56.280
we've got their back.

00:07:56.280 --> 00:07:59.460
So I'm going to talk a little
bit more in three areas

00:07:59.460 --> 00:08:01.080
around device management.

00:08:01.080 --> 00:08:04.890
And I'm going to give a demo
in each one of these areas.

00:08:04.890 --> 00:08:07.840
So, hopefully, we'll be
ready to go on these.

00:08:07.840 --> 00:08:12.660
So, first, let's talk about
comprehensive protection.

00:08:12.660 --> 00:08:17.460
So, on the mobile side,
we manage iOS and Android.

00:08:17.460 --> 00:08:21.900
We manage company-owned devices,
we manage personal devices.

00:08:21.900 --> 00:08:25.890
We also help secure the desktop
through the use of an Endpoint

00:08:25.890 --> 00:08:27.698
Verification Chrome extension.

00:08:27.698 --> 00:08:29.490
That's something that
we launched last year

00:08:29.490 --> 00:08:32.490
and talked about it
at Next last year.

00:08:32.490 --> 00:08:34.049
We curate and
install G Suite apps.

00:08:34.049 --> 00:08:36.049
We're going to talk a
little bit more about that

00:08:36.049 --> 00:08:37.840
and show a demo a
little bit later.

00:08:37.840 --> 00:08:40.710
And we, also, are big fans
of Android Enterprise.

00:08:40.710 --> 00:08:45.000
And we support Android work
profiles to allow selected data

00:08:45.000 --> 00:08:47.250
control, where you can
completely separate out

00:08:47.250 --> 00:08:49.873
your work and your
personal data on a device,

00:08:49.873 --> 00:08:51.540
so that you don't
bleed between the two.

00:08:54.900 --> 00:08:57.330
Today, we are launching
a new feature.

00:08:57.330 --> 00:09:00.190
It's Drive File
Stream management.

00:09:00.190 --> 00:09:03.180
So, if you're not familiar
with Drive File Stream,

00:09:03.180 --> 00:09:05.610
it's the world's
largest hard drive.

00:09:05.610 --> 00:09:09.810
So, Drive File Stream allows
you to link up your Cloud drive

00:09:09.810 --> 00:09:12.240
to your Windows or
your Mac machine.

00:09:12.240 --> 00:09:16.560
It looks just like a regular
hard drive on your device.

00:09:16.560 --> 00:09:18.330
It automatically syncs
in the background

00:09:18.330 --> 00:09:19.948
the files that you need.

00:09:19.948 --> 00:09:21.990
The first thing that you
hear from administrators

00:09:21.990 --> 00:09:23.520
is, we love this
feature but we want

00:09:23.520 --> 00:09:24.840
to make sure that
we have control over

00:09:24.840 --> 00:09:26.790
that if the device
becomes lost or stolen.

00:09:26.790 --> 00:09:31.390
We want to make sure that we
can take care of that device.

00:09:31.390 --> 00:09:34.150
So, today, we are launching
management of that device.

00:09:34.150 --> 00:09:36.210
We can limit Drive
File Stream access

00:09:36.210 --> 00:09:38.010
to only company-owned devices.

00:09:38.010 --> 00:09:41.100
And should that device
become lost or stolen,

00:09:41.100 --> 00:09:44.610
we can wipe that device
and/or block that device.

00:09:44.610 --> 00:09:46.200
And to show you--

00:09:46.200 --> 00:09:49.918
thanks-- and to show you
a demo of that today,

00:09:49.918 --> 00:09:51.960
I'm going to pass it over
to my colleague Sachin.

00:09:51.960 --> 00:09:53.258
Sachin.

00:09:53.258 --> 00:09:55.050
