Great, so, next one.

00:14:03.660 --> 00:14:05.035
Let's talk about
easy management,

00:14:05.035 --> 00:14:08.540
and then we'll come back up
for another quick demo, here.

00:14:08.540 --> 00:14:11.560
So I talked a little bit
about our growth and device

00:14:11.560 --> 00:14:12.280
management.

00:14:12.280 --> 00:14:14.238
So one of the things that
we've been focused on

00:14:14.238 --> 00:14:16.510
is what is the end
user experience

00:14:16.510 --> 00:14:17.740
for device management.

00:14:17.740 --> 00:14:19.630
I think that a lot
of people hesitate

00:14:19.630 --> 00:14:23.285
to turn on management because
people have to jump through too

00:14:23.285 --> 00:14:24.160
many hoops, you know?

00:14:24.160 --> 00:14:27.100
They don't want a ton of calls
to their help center on how

00:14:27.100 --> 00:14:29.180
to set up and get access
to Gmail, and Drive,

00:14:29.180 --> 00:14:31.430
and Calendar and
that sort of thing.

00:14:31.430 --> 00:14:34.540
So, what we implemented was
agentless basic management

00:14:34.540 --> 00:14:37.810
for all of our G Suite and
Cloud Identity customers.

00:14:37.810 --> 00:14:40.690
What that gets them is an
inventory of all the devices

00:14:40.690 --> 00:14:43.390
that access their accounts.

00:14:43.390 --> 00:14:46.750
It allows them to enforce a
passcode on those devices,

00:14:46.750 --> 00:14:49.060
and it allows them
to selectively wipe

00:14:49.060 --> 00:14:50.560
the accounts on those devices.

00:14:50.560 --> 00:14:52.420
And it's important to
note that it allows

00:14:52.420 --> 00:14:54.400
them to wipe the
accounts on the devices,

00:14:54.400 --> 00:14:56.120
but not the devices themselves.

00:14:56.120 --> 00:14:58.540
So it means that the
administrator has control

00:14:58.540 --> 00:15:01.067
over the corporate
data on the device,

00:15:01.067 --> 00:15:03.400
but they're not going to be
able to wipe somebody's baby

00:15:03.400 --> 00:15:07.120
pictures off of that device.

00:15:07.120 --> 00:15:09.640
We can do this without
installing an agent

00:15:09.640 --> 00:15:10.390
on the device.

00:15:10.390 --> 00:15:13.930
Just the act of, say,
logging into Gmail

00:15:13.930 --> 00:15:15.970
enables this device management.

00:15:15.970 --> 00:15:19.030
We turned this on by default
for all of our new G Suite

00:15:19.030 --> 00:15:21.740
customers about a year ago.

00:15:21.740 --> 00:15:24.520
And we've been
slowly retroactively

00:15:24.520 --> 00:15:26.800
applying it to all of our
existing G Suite customers.

00:15:26.800 --> 00:15:28.790
We haven't finished
that rollout yet,

00:15:28.790 --> 00:15:30.980
but we're in the
process of doing that.

00:15:30.980 --> 00:15:33.160
So it's a very easy
way to get management

00:15:33.160 --> 00:15:35.410
of every single device--
every single mobile device--

00:15:35.410 --> 00:15:39.490
that touches G Suite and
Cloud Identity accounts.

00:15:39.490 --> 00:15:42.460
We're really pleased with this
because what this means is--

00:15:42.460 --> 00:15:44.890
even the administrators
who haven't figured out yet

00:15:44.890 --> 00:15:47.260
that device management is
very important to them--

00:15:47.260 --> 00:15:49.700
the first time that someone
leaves their mobile device

00:15:49.700 --> 00:15:53.430
that's filled with health care
data or social security numbers

00:15:53.430 --> 00:15:56.920
in a Starbucks unprotected,
we've got their back.

00:15:56.920 --> 00:15:59.740
They can go and they can wipe
the account off that device.

00:15:59.740 --> 00:16:01.870
So, very pleased with that.

00:16:01.870 --> 00:16:05.710
For those enterprises that
need more bells and whistles

00:16:05.710 --> 00:16:07.690
and more policy
enforcement, we do

00:16:07.690 --> 00:16:09.550
provide more advanced controls.

00:16:09.550 --> 00:16:10.990
In that case, it's
not agentless,

00:16:10.990 --> 00:16:12.907
you have to install a
Device Policy controller

00:16:12.907 --> 00:16:13.570
on the device.

00:16:13.570 --> 00:16:18.070
But we do work very hard to
make that as easy as possible.

00:16:18.070 --> 00:16:20.560
And so, you know,
what I like to say

00:16:20.560 --> 00:16:24.550
is that, you're all here
today at Google Cloud Next

00:16:24.550 --> 00:16:27.580
to talk to us about
enterprise software.

00:16:27.580 --> 00:16:31.060
You may be aware that Google
also has some consumer brands

00:16:31.060 --> 00:16:33.250
that we work on, as well.

00:16:33.250 --> 00:16:36.940
So what we do is, we
take some of the lessons

00:16:36.940 --> 00:16:39.160
that we've learned from
providing great consumer

00:16:39.160 --> 00:16:41.290
experiences to
billions of users.

00:16:41.290 --> 00:16:43.970
And we try to apply that to
enterprise software, as well.

00:16:43.970 --> 00:16:47.590
And so, even in the places where
we might require our end users

00:16:47.590 --> 00:16:49.150
to jump through
a few extra hoops

00:16:49.150 --> 00:16:52.250
to get more advanced
management on their devices,

00:16:52.250 --> 00:16:54.190
we really focus on the
end user experience

00:16:54.190 --> 00:16:56.920
and try to make that as
seamless as possible so

00:16:56.920 --> 00:16:59.050
that the administrator
doesn't get burdened

00:16:59.050 --> 00:17:01.450
with a lot of support
calls, and the end user

00:17:01.450 --> 00:17:04.045
knows exactly what is
happening on that device.

00:17:04.045 --> 00:17:05.920
We're completely
transparent about the rights

00:17:05.920 --> 00:17:07.780
that the administrator
has on that device.

00:17:07.780 --> 00:17:10.952
And, ultimately, they end
up installing, and getting

00:17:10.952 --> 00:17:12.369
on their way, and
being productive

00:17:12.369 --> 00:17:16.619
in whatever they need to do with
that enterprise information.

00:17:16.619 --> 00:17:19.430
And then, finally, we also
spend some time thinking

00:17:19.430 --> 00:17:21.500
about how do we make
the admin's life easier.

00:17:21.500 --> 00:17:23.250
And so one of the
things we've implemented

00:17:23.250 --> 00:17:27.190
are customizable rules
on the admin side,

00:17:27.190 --> 00:17:29.000
so you can implement--

00:17:29.000 --> 00:17:32.780
or automate-- everyday tasks
on the administrator side.

00:17:32.780 --> 00:17:36.380
So things like, oh, I want to
lock down a device if someone

00:17:36.380 --> 00:17:38.640
tries to log in
with the passcode,

00:17:38.640 --> 00:17:40.780
you know, incorrectly
five times in a row.

00:17:40.780 --> 00:17:43.280
I might automatically trigger
an account wipe on that device

00:17:43.280 --> 00:17:44.572
to protect data on that device.

00:17:47.120 --> 00:17:50.250
So, today, I want to show
you-- we actually launched this

00:17:50.250 --> 00:17:51.860
a couple of months ago--

00:17:51.860 --> 00:17:56.020
but we've extended the agentless
management concept on Android

00:17:56.020 --> 00:17:58.040
to include application
management.

00:17:58.040 --> 00:18:00.850
So in addition to
inventorying the device,

00:18:00.850 --> 00:18:03.220
being able to enforce a
passcode on the device,

00:18:03.220 --> 00:18:07.490
and being able to wipe the
account on the device--

00:18:07.490 --> 00:18:11.530
we also, on Android, can
whitelist applications.

00:18:11.530 --> 00:18:13.870
And push those applications
to Android devices

00:18:13.870 --> 00:18:16.180
without the need to
install a Device Policy

00:18:16.180 --> 00:18:17.450
controller on the device.

00:18:17.450 --> 00:18:19.075
And so, once again,
we're going to have

00:18:19.075 --> 00:18:20.410
Sachin show us a demo of that.

