00:25:07.990 --> 00:25:09.050
as well.

00:25:09.050 --> 00:25:11.200
We provide proactive
security recommendations

00:25:11.200 --> 00:25:14.740
for device management with our
Security Center integration.

00:25:14.740 --> 00:25:18.220
And, just recently,
Android Enterprise

00:25:18.220 --> 00:25:21.970
launched the Android Enterprise
Recommended program for EMMs.

00:25:21.970 --> 00:25:23.590
This is in this
past January and we

00:25:23.590 --> 00:25:27.197
were one of the launch
partners for that.

00:25:27.197 --> 00:25:29.030
One of the features
that we're talking about

00:25:29.030 --> 00:25:31.880
is-- it's not quite launched
yet, but it's coming soon.

00:25:31.880 --> 00:25:35.710
So, I mentioned earlier, we're
fans of Android Enterprise

00:25:35.710 --> 00:25:37.460
and the Android work
profile-- the ability

00:25:37.460 --> 00:25:41.090
to completely separate out work
and personal data on an Android

00:25:41.090 --> 00:25:41.970
phone.

00:25:41.970 --> 00:25:43.610
I say that not
just because we're

00:25:43.610 --> 00:25:45.920
Google and Android's
Google, although maybe.

00:25:45.920 --> 00:25:49.040
But also because they've really
thought through the enterprise

00:25:49.040 --> 00:25:49.590
use case.

00:25:49.590 --> 00:25:53.300
And it's really easy, as an EMM,
for us to kind of flip a switch

00:25:53.300 --> 00:25:56.460
and separate out work and
personal data on a device.

00:25:56.460 --> 00:25:58.757
It's not quite as easy
to do that on iOS.

00:25:58.757 --> 00:26:00.590
But we recognize that
a lot of our customers

00:26:00.590 --> 00:26:03.650
have a need to provide
enterprise support on iOS.

00:26:03.650 --> 00:26:06.610
And so one of the features that
we will be launching soon--

00:26:06.610 --> 00:26:08.150
and we have in testing now--

00:26:08.150 --> 00:26:10.010
is the data
exfiltration protection

00:26:10.010 --> 00:26:13.790
for iOS, which does things
like restrict copy/paste

00:26:13.790 --> 00:26:18.050
between corporate and personal
accounts on an iOS device.

00:26:18.050 --> 00:26:20.900
So watch for more announcements
to come on this in the coming

00:26:20.900 --> 00:26:23.678
months, throughout the year.

00:26:23.678 --> 00:26:25.470
I want to talk a little
bit about something

00:26:25.470 --> 00:26:28.590
you should hear a lot about
throughout this conference,

00:26:28.590 --> 00:26:30.420
context-aware access.

00:26:30.420 --> 00:26:33.780
So, kind of, traditional
mobile management

00:26:33.780 --> 00:26:36.870
and traditional device
management has, in the past,

00:26:36.870 --> 00:26:39.330
been a very binary thing, right?

00:26:39.330 --> 00:26:42.570
Which is, you put a
Device Policy controller

00:26:42.570 --> 00:26:45.330
into the device, it
makes sure that policies

00:26:45.330 --> 00:26:47.230
are enforced on that device.

00:26:47.230 --> 00:26:50.280
And it's either compliant
or not compliant,

00:26:50.280 --> 00:26:52.090
and then you allow access.

00:26:52.090 --> 00:26:55.240
And that model is not
going to continue to work,

00:26:55.240 --> 00:26:57.960
because there are many
different signals that you need

00:26:57.960 --> 00:27:00.540
to look at on a device to
determine whether you should

00:27:00.540 --> 00:27:01.420
allow access.

00:27:01.420 --> 00:27:03.150
And those signals
change over time,

00:27:03.150 --> 00:27:05.490
because they change
depending on the user that's

00:27:05.490 --> 00:27:06.810
logged into the device.

00:27:06.810 --> 00:27:08.940
They change depending
on the applications that

00:27:08.940 --> 00:27:10.542
are being used on the device.

00:27:10.542 --> 00:27:12.750
They change depending on
the environmental variables,

00:27:12.750 --> 00:27:16.110
like what network the device is
on, what country the device is

00:27:16.110 --> 00:27:17.630
located on, et cetera.

00:27:17.630 --> 00:27:19.440
And context-aware
access is something

00:27:19.440 --> 00:27:24.180
that we were working on to
support those types of security

00:27:24.180 --> 00:27:25.387
scenarios.

00:27:25.387 --> 00:27:26.970
There's more to come
in other sessions

00:27:26.970 --> 00:27:27.960
and I'm going to
have a slide, here,

00:27:27.960 --> 00:27:30.418
that will show you some of the
sessions you should go watch

00:27:30.418 --> 00:27:31.900
to see about that.

00:27:31.900 --> 00:27:36.990
So what we are launching
in beta is the ability

00:27:36.990 --> 00:27:41.040
to take this BeyondCorp
Devices API--

00:27:41.040 --> 00:27:44.700
will allow us to integrate
with third parties

00:27:44.700 --> 00:27:51.240
to ingest additional
signals from other companies

00:27:51.240 --> 00:27:56.560
to supplement that context-aware
access rules engine.

00:27:56.560 --> 00:27:59.850
So what we look at is,
we work with partners,

00:27:59.850 --> 00:28:02.780
partners send us
data, on oh, here

00:28:02.780 --> 00:28:04.807
are the types of apps
that we see installed

00:28:04.807 --> 00:28:06.390
on the device that
might be malicious,

00:28:06.390 --> 00:28:08.970
or other things, other
environmental variables.

00:28:08.970 --> 00:28:10.720
And we put those into
our rules engine,

00:28:10.720 --> 00:28:12.512
and an administrator
can make a decision on

00:28:12.512 --> 00:28:13.980
whether they want
to grant access

00:28:13.980 --> 00:28:18.280
to corporate data on
those devices or not.

00:28:18.280 --> 00:28:20.830
And so what we want to do is,
rather than tell you about it,

00:28:20.830 --> 00:28:23.400
we wanted to bring up a
partner to talk about it.

00:28:23.400 --> 00:28:25.742
So Sneha, from Lookout, is
going to come up and tell you

00:28:25.742 --> 00:28:27.450
a little bit about
integration that we've

00:28:27.450 --> 00:28:28.860
built with Lookout for this.

00:28:33.990 --> 00:28:36.120
SNEHA SACHIDANANDA: Thanks
for having me, Brad.

00:28:36.120 --> 00:28:37.630
So, hi, everyone.

00:28:37.630 --> 00:28:39.450
My name is Sneha
Sachidananda, I'm

00:28:39.450 --> 00:28:43.140
a senior product
manager at Lookout.

00:28:43.140 --> 00:28:44.880
So I want to give
you some context.

00:28:44.880 --> 00:28:48.760
As we all know, today, employees
are working from everywhere.

00:28:48.760 --> 00:28:51.150
We are working from
coffee shops, airports,

00:28:51.150 --> 00:28:52.500
movie theaters.

00:28:52.500 --> 00:28:56.820
And we are always accessing
our corporate data like email,

00:28:56.820 --> 00:29:01.060
our Docs, Slides, et
cetera on mobile devices.

00:29:01.060 --> 00:29:03.720
So that poses a
pretty big challenges

00:29:03.720 --> 00:29:05.820
for enterprises
who have completely

00:29:05.820 --> 00:29:09.490
embraced mobile devices
throughout their workplace.

00:29:09.490 --> 00:29:12.320
So, how can they ensure
that corporate data which

00:29:12.320 --> 00:29:15.090
is being accessed
from these devices

00:29:15.090 --> 00:29:18.000
are actually happening
in a secure fashion?

00:29:18.000 --> 00:29:19.620
And also, at the
same time, they have

00:29:19.620 --> 00:29:23.790
to balance the user privacy,
as well as not disrupt

00:29:23.790 --> 00:29:25.450
the user experience.

00:29:25.450 --> 00:29:28.170
So, today, I'm here
to talk about how

00:29:28.170 --> 00:29:30.780
we are going to do that with
Lookout and Google Cloud

00:29:30.780 --> 00:29:32.010
Identity.

00:29:32.010 --> 00:29:33.400
Next slide.

00:29:33.400 --> 00:29:37.440
So for those of you who have
not heard about Lookout--

00:29:37.440 --> 00:29:39.570
Lookout Mobile
Endpoint Security,

00:29:39.570 --> 00:29:42.780
we help enterprises
protect corporate data

00:29:42.780 --> 00:29:46.050
by preventing any kind of
threats that can happen

00:29:46.050 --> 00:29:48.520
on employee mobile devices.

00:29:48.520 --> 00:29:50.820
We have been doing
this over a decade,

00:29:50.820 --> 00:29:55.800
and we have protected over
more than 100 million devices

00:29:55.800 --> 00:29:57.910
across 150 countries.

00:29:57.910 --> 00:30:00.780
And for enterprises,
we actually protect

00:30:00.780 --> 00:30:03.390
across a spectrum of risks.

00:30:03.390 --> 00:30:07.690
And on a broad range, we divide
them into four categories.

00:30:07.690 --> 00:30:11.760
So, the first category that we
have is called app-based risks.

00:30:11.760 --> 00:30:15.090
So, an example of
this can be a malware

00:30:15.090 --> 00:30:17.580
that can be either downloaded
from Play Store or even side

00:30:17.580 --> 00:30:19.510
loaded on an employee device.

00:30:19.510 --> 00:30:22.470
An example of zero-day
malware that Lookout detected

00:30:22.470 --> 00:30:24.510
is a Pegasus, iOS spyware.

00:30:24.510 --> 00:30:26.460
Some of you may have
heard of that one.

00:30:26.460 --> 00:30:28.570
And we have device-based risk.

00:30:28.570 --> 00:30:32.340
So an example of this
can be when a device

00:30:32.340 --> 00:30:35.820
could get rooted or jailbroken.

00:30:35.820 --> 00:30:38.970
We are shortly going to
show you a demo of that.

00:30:38.970 --> 00:30:40.660
And then we have
network-based risk,

00:30:40.660 --> 00:30:42.790
where you could connect
to an unsafe Wi-Fi.

00:30:42.790 --> 00:30:44.707
There could be-- a
man-in-the-middle attack is

00:30:44.707 --> 00:30:45.730
an example of that.

00:30:45.730 --> 00:30:49.230
And finally, phishing attack
can happen when an employee

00:30:49.230 --> 00:30:51.270
could click on an email--

00:30:51.270 --> 00:30:53.800
a malicious email-- which could
be either as a text message,

00:30:53.800 --> 00:30:56.010
or even on a social media
platform like WhatsApp.

00:30:56.010 --> 00:30:58.790
So what we do is
when anytime there

00:30:58.790 --> 00:31:02.230
is a risk detected on any
of these four categories,

00:31:02.230 --> 00:31:04.110
we provide protection
for corporate data

00:31:04.110 --> 00:31:07.720
through what we call it as
continuous conditional access.

00:31:07.720 --> 00:31:08.820
Next slide.

00:31:08.820 --> 00:31:12.370
So for this integration with
the Lookout and Google Cloud

00:31:12.370 --> 00:31:14.670
Identity, what we
have done is we

00:31:14.670 --> 00:31:18.630
have integrated our device
held data with the BeyondCorp

00:31:18.630 --> 00:31:20.160
Devices API.

00:31:20.160 --> 00:31:24.660
So when an end user is trying
to access a corporate resource--

00:31:24.660 --> 00:31:27.660
like, let's say G Suite--
on their mobile device,

00:31:27.660 --> 00:31:29.430
in real time we
will give context

00:31:29.430 --> 00:31:31.270
about the health of the device.

00:31:31.270 --> 00:31:35.130
So once we get to know what
the context of the device

00:31:35.130 --> 00:31:39.960
is, then you can apply all
of these rules engines,

00:31:39.960 --> 00:31:42.120
as well as you can apply
different enforcement

00:31:42.120 --> 00:31:46.230
policy as an admin, to make
sure that only safe devices get

00:31:46.230 --> 00:31:48.820
access to the
corporate resources.

00:31:48.820 --> 00:31:51.150
So without giving
too much away, I

00:31:51.150 --> 00:31:54.120
would like to invite
Atul Tulshibagwale

00:31:54.120 --> 00:31:58.605
Google, who will actually show
a demo of this integration.

00:31:58.605 --> 00:31:59.980
ATUL TULSHIBAGWALE:
Hi, everyone.

00:31:59.980 --> 00:32:01.110
Thanks, Sneha.

00:32:01.110 --> 00:32:03.780
So, I'm going to show you a
demo today of the Devices API

00:32:03.780 --> 00:32:06.060
and how a third
party, like Lookout,

00:32:06.060 --> 00:32:09.600
can use the Devices
API to enforce

00:32:09.600 --> 00:32:13.440
greater security on the devices
that are managed by Google.

00:32:18.290 --> 00:32:20.100
Oh, yes.

00:32:20.100 --> 00:32:23.370
So, here, I have a
device that there

00:32:23.370 --> 00:32:24.540
is no agent on this device.

00:32:24.540 --> 00:32:26.490
This is just a simple
device on which

00:32:26.490 --> 00:32:27.850
I have my personal account.

00:32:27.850 --> 00:32:30.830
I'm calling myself Harry
Potter and I'm going

00:32:30.830 --> 00:32:33.420
to add my work account, here.

00:32:33.420 --> 00:32:35.520
And I've just started a
new job at this company

00:32:35.520 --> 00:32:36.187
called Terragon.

00:32:38.550 --> 00:32:48.440
And I'm logging
into the device, I'm

00:32:48.440 --> 00:32:50.900
adding my corporate
account now which

00:32:50.900 --> 00:32:52.940
is harrypotter@terragon.tv.

00:32:55.790 --> 00:32:58.100
I need to enter my
password, of course.

00:32:58.100 --> 00:33:06.807
So that's my password
and oh, do they actually

00:33:06.807 --> 00:33:07.640
show it to everyone?

00:33:07.640 --> 00:33:10.160
Sorry.

00:33:10.160 --> 00:33:11.330
I'm here, I'm in.

00:33:11.330 --> 00:33:14.060
I see my work email here.

00:33:14.060 --> 00:33:17.570
And my boss has just sent
me this new document.

00:33:17.570 --> 00:33:21.470
Oh, OK, well, I thought there
was an interesting email

00:33:21.470 --> 00:33:22.550
in my personal account.

00:33:22.550 --> 00:33:26.030
But before I go there, I'm
told to activate the Lookout

00:33:26.030 --> 00:33:26.730
application.

00:33:26.730 --> 00:33:29.060
So I'm going to
activate it here.

00:33:29.060 --> 00:33:37.820
So activate, enter my
work email address.

00:33:37.820 --> 00:33:43.520
And what Lookout
is now doing is,

00:33:43.520 --> 00:33:45.740
it's basically
scanning my device.

00:33:45.740 --> 00:33:49.433
I need to give it the permission
so that it can do its job.

00:33:49.433 --> 00:33:50.850
You know, it's
scanning my device,

00:33:50.850 --> 00:33:53.100
it's making sure that
everything is OK with my device,

00:33:53.100 --> 00:33:54.530
and it's analyzing things.

00:33:54.530 --> 00:33:58.040
Now, in the meantime, I can show
you on the Google Admin console

00:33:58.040 --> 00:34:02.540
that the device has been
registered and approved

00:34:02.540 --> 00:34:03.980
without an agent.

00:34:03.980 --> 00:34:07.700
And on the Lookout
console, here, you'll

00:34:07.700 --> 00:34:14.020
be able to see that the device
is connected and secured.

00:34:14.020 --> 00:34:17.469
And so, if you go back
to the device screen,

00:34:17.469 --> 00:34:20.550
you see that Lookout
found no issues here.

00:34:20.550 --> 00:34:22.300
So, what I'm going to
do now, is I'm going

00:34:22.300 --> 00:34:25.900
to go to my personal email.

00:34:25.900 --> 00:34:28.210
Oh, there's a application
for the university

00:34:28.210 --> 00:34:30.219
I was wanting to apply to.

00:34:30.219 --> 00:34:32.050
So, I'm going to
click on this link

00:34:32.050 --> 00:34:33.489
so that I get that application.

00:34:33.489 --> 00:34:36.920
And it's giving me some language
that I don't really understand.

00:34:36.920 --> 00:34:38.739
So I'm just going to
click on that link

00:34:38.739 --> 00:34:41.717
and it's going to
download this file.

00:34:41.717 --> 00:34:43.300
I don't know what
that file is, but it

00:34:43.300 --> 00:34:46.130
looks like if I download it,
I get into the university.

00:34:46.130 --> 00:34:48.610
So, I'm going to do that.

00:34:48.610 --> 00:34:52.750
And, you know, behold, this
is Lookout basically getting

00:34:52.750 --> 00:34:56.710
triggered and saying that
the device is now, you know,

00:34:56.710 --> 00:34:58.400
this file is harmful.

00:34:58.400 --> 00:35:00.670
And I should not basically,
do anything with it.

00:35:00.670 --> 00:35:01.840
I'm going to close it.

00:35:01.840 --> 00:35:05.440
But just because the file was
downloaded on this device,

00:35:05.440 --> 00:35:09.340
now you will see that,
in the Gmail client,

00:35:09.340 --> 00:35:12.230
the work account
has been eliminated.

00:35:12.230 --> 00:35:14.350
So if I had downloaded
any files or anything,

00:35:14.350 --> 00:35:16.090
all those would be gone.

00:35:16.090 --> 00:35:19.870
And how this happened,
actually, is because--

00:35:19.870 --> 00:35:23.320
on the Google Admin
console, the administrator

00:35:23.320 --> 00:35:24.380
had configured a rule.

00:35:24.380 --> 00:35:30.250
Now, this is under Google
Admin, you see a Rules tab.

00:35:30.250 --> 00:35:32.710
And within that
there is a rule here,

00:35:32.710 --> 00:35:35.890
that if a partner
signals, you know,

00:35:35.890 --> 00:35:38.350
that there is a high
risk on the device, then

00:35:38.350 --> 00:35:41.210
you should wipe the corporate
account from that device.

00:35:41.210 --> 00:35:46.853
And so if the basic integrity
sees that there's a high risk,

00:35:46.853 --> 00:35:49.020
we should wipe the corporate
account on that device.

00:35:49.020 --> 00:35:50.000
And that's the rule.

00:35:50.000 --> 00:35:51.850
And if you look
at the audit logs,

00:35:51.850 --> 00:36:01.640
you will see that the latest
audit logs will show--

00:36:01.640 --> 00:36:03.620
this is at 1:20 PM--

00:36:03.620 --> 00:36:06.710
that the risk was changed
from none to high,

00:36:06.710 --> 00:36:10.070
and then the account wipe was
pending, and then executed.

00:36:10.070 --> 00:36:12.290
So this happened without
the user actually

00:36:12.290 --> 00:36:13.598
having to do anything.

00:36:13.598 --> 00:36:15.140
And this shows how
we can collaborate

00:36:15.140 --> 00:36:17.542
with other partners.

00:36:17.542 --> 00:36:18.474
Thank you.

00:36:18.474 --> 00:36:20.984
[APPLAUSE]

00:36:20.984 --> 00:36:22.484
BRAD MEADER: Great,
thank you, Atul.

